    pyqs: [
      { year: 2025, marks: 1, section: 'Section A', unit: 'Unit 5', frequency: 'Compulsory 1M', isImportant: true, question: 'Why does UDP have less overhead than TCP?', answer: 'UDP has an 8-byte header compared to TCP\'s 20-byte minimum header, and it does not maintain connection state, acknowledgments, retransmissions, or flow/congestion control mechanisms.', examTip: 'Compare 8-byte vs 20-byte header sizes.' },
      { year: 2025, marks: 2, section: 'Section B', unit: 'Unit 3', frequency: 'Compulsory 2M', isImportant: true, question: 'Why is there a minimum frame size requirement in CSMA/CD (Ethernet)?', answer: 'To ensure that a transmitting station does not finish sending the frame before the collision signal from the farthest end of the cable travels back to it. Hence, Transmission Time (T_trans) must be at least twice the Propagation Delay (2 * T_prop).', examTip: 'Formula: Length >= 2 * T_prop * Bandwidth.' },
      { year: 2025, marks: 5, section: 'Section C', unit: 'Unit 2', frequency: 'MTT 5M', isImportant: true, question: 'A bit stream 1101011011 is transmitted using CRC with generator polynomial G(x) = x^4 + x + 1. Calculate the transmitted frame.', answer: '1. Generator polynomial G(x) = x^4 + 0*x^3 + 0*x^2 + 1*x^1 + 1 = 10011 (Degree = 4).\n2. Append 4 zeros to bit stream: 11010110110000.\n3. Modulo-2 binary division (using XOR):\n11010110110000 divided by 10011 yields Quotient 1100001010 and Remainder 1110.\n4. Replace the 4 appended zeros with the 4-bit remainder (Checksum = 1110).\nTransmitted Frame = 11010110111110.', examTip: 'Show long division with XOR subtractions step by step.' },
      { year: 2025, marks: 10, section: 'Section D', unit: 'Unit 4', frequency: 'MTT -> ETT 10M (High Weightage)', isImportant: true, question: 'An organization is granted the block 192.168.10.0/24. The administrator needs to create 4 subnets: Subnet 1 (60 hosts), Subnet 2 (60 hosts), Subnet 3 (30 hosts), Subnet 4 (30 hosts). Design the subnets specifying Subnet Mask, Network Address, Usable Host Range, and Broadcast Address for each subnet.', answer: 'Base network: 192.168.10.0/24 (Total 256 addresses).\n\n1. Subnet 1 (60 hosts needed):\n- Host bits needed: 2^h - 2 >= 60 -> h = 6 (2^6 = 64 addresses).\n- Prefix = 32 - 6 = /26. Subnet Mask = 255.255.255.192.\n- Network Address: 192.168.10.0/26\n- Usable Host Range: 192.168.10.1 to 192.168.10.62\n- Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.10.63\n\n2. Subnet 2 (60 hosts needed):\n- Host bits needed: h = 6 -> Prefix /26. Subnet Mask = 255.255.255.192.\n- Network Address: 192.168.10.64/26\n- Usable Host Range: 192.168.10.65 to 192.168.10.126\n- Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.10.127\n\n3. Subnet 3 (30 hosts needed):\n- Host bits needed: 2^h - 2 >= 30 -> h = 5 (2^5 = 32 addresses).\n- Prefix = 32 - 5 = /27. Subnet Mask = 255.255.255.224.\n- Network Address: 192.168.10.128/27\n- Usable Host Range: 192.168.10.129 to 192.168.10.158\n- Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.10.159\n\n4. Subnet 4 (30 hosts needed):\n- Host bits needed: h = 5 -> Prefix /27. Subnet Mask = 255.255.255.224.\n- Network Address: 192.168.10.160/27\n- Usable Host Range: 192.168.10.161 to 192.168.10.190\n- Directed Broadcast Address: 192.168.10.191.', examTip: 'Use a neat summary table with columns: Subnet | Mask | Net ID | First Host | Last Host | Broadcast.' },
      { year: 2024, marks: 10, section: 'Section D', unit: 'Unit 5', frequency: 'ETT 10M', isImportant: true, question: 'Explain TCP Congestion Control mechanisms in detail. Describe Slow Start, Congestion Avoidance, Fast Retransmit, and Fast Recovery. Draw the Congestion Window (cwnd) vs Transmission Round curve.', answer: '1. Overview: TCP manages congestion by dynamically adjusting Congestion Window (cwnd) based on perceived network capacity.\n2. Phases:\n- Phase 1: Slow Start: cwnd starts at 1 MSS. For every ACK received, cwnd increases by 1 MSS (effectively doubling cwnd every RTT: 1, 2, 4, 8...). Continues until cwnd reaches `ssthresh` (Slow Start Threshold).\n- Phase 2: Congestion Avoidance: Once cwnd >= ssthresh, exponential growth transitions to linear growth (Additive Increase): cwnd increases by 1 MSS per RTT (AIMD policy).\n- Phase 3: Packet Loss via Timeout: High congestion detected. ssthresh is set to cwnd / 2, and cwnd drops all the way back to 1 MSS, restarting Slow Start.\n- Phase 4: Packet Loss via 3 Duplicate ACKs (Fast Retransmit & Fast Recovery): TCP immediately retransmits the missing segment without waiting for timeout. Enters Fast Recovery: ssthresh set to cwnd / 2, and cwnd set to ssthresh + 3 MSS, resuming linear Congestion Avoidance without resetting to 1.\n3. Diagram: Graph showing cwnd (y-axis) vs RTT rounds (x-axis) with exponential curve up to threshold, linear climbing, sharp drop on timeout vs halving on triple duplicate ACK.', examTip: 'Differentiate between Timeout (drops to 1) and 3 Duplicate ACKs (halves to ssthresh).' },
    ],
